{
  "record_id": "036",
  "problem": "Optimal circuit distance of the Gross code",
  "collection": "Original research investigation",
  "summary": "Agreement of two pairwise precedence comparisons is strictly weaker than separating the two checks in time. The step after 'i.e.' changes the ordering predicate.",
  "review_status": "source_reviewed",
  "review_note": "No open interpretation blocker was recorded in the preceding source review. This is not a new independent mathematical verification.",
  "original": {
    "excerpt": "1. **For the gross code, (MC) *is* [SBB26]'s non-interleaving condition.** Every overlapping\n   (X,Z) check pair shares exactly two qubits (§1), so `c_ab ∈ {0,1,2}`, and `c_ab` even means the\n   two shared CNOT pairs agree on their order, i.e. either all of `a`'s CNOTs on the overlap precede\n   all of `b`'s, or vice versa — S5 verbatim. **CONJECTURED** (the derivation is one line given §1;\n   the risk is only in whether S5's \"either…or\" is per-pair or global — S6 forces per-pair, since a\n   low-depth LRC cannot satisfy a global split).",
    "context": "For overlapping X and Z checks, measurement consistency (MC) requires an even number of shared data qubits with Z-before-X local ordering. The draft identifies this pairwise parity condition with non-interleaving, which requires all operations of one check on their common support to precede all operations of the other. The proposed equivalence was labelled CONJECTURED.",
    "locator": "Proof draft dated 20 August 2026, section 2.4(1), lines 246–251."
  },
  "witness": {
    "explanation": "Agreement of two pairwise precedence comparisons is strictly weaker than separating the two checks in time. The step after 'i.e.' changes the ordering predicate.",
    "evidence": "Take common-support gate times A_1=1, B_1=2, A_2=3, B_2=4. Both comparisons B_i<A_i are false, so the MC count is zero (even), but neither max(A)<min(B) nor max(B)<min(A) holds. The archived Gross-specific witness uses time arrays LX(left)=[11,10,3], LX(right)=[7,5,8], LZ(left)=[6,8,5], LZ(right)=[2,1,10]. It has zero MC violations among 648 overlaps, while 288 pairs are interleaved. The elementary four-time example independently refutes the inference from pairwise order agreement to non-interleaving.",
    "scope": "tentative_candidate_refuted"
  },
  "archived_witness_excerpt": "**The equivalence is false.** The target's derivation (§2.4(1)) reads: \"`c_ab` even means the two\nshared CNOT pairs agree on their order, i.e. either all of `a`'s CNOTs on the overlap precede all\nof `b`'s, or vice versa — S5 verbatim.\" The step after \"i.e.\" is a non-sequitur. With shared times\n`A₁,A₂` for `a` and `B₁,B₂` for `b`, \"agree\" is `(B₁<A₁) ⟺ (B₂<A₂)`; \"all before all\" is\n`max(A) < min(B)` or `max(B) < min(A)`. `A₁=1 < B₁=2 < A₂=3 < B₂=4` agrees but is interleaved.\n\nThis is not a hypothetical. Searching legal 12-layer translation-invariant gross schedules I found\none in seconds:\n\n```\nLX(left)=[11,10,3]  LX(right)=[7,5,8]  LZ(left)=[6,8,5]  LZ(right)=[2,1,10]\nlegal (all four 6-element unions distinct);  (MC) violations 0 / 648\nSBB non-interleaving violated on 4 of the 9 monomial classes  = 288 / 648 pairs\n```\n\nSo **(MC) ⇏ non-interleaved**. The converse *does* hold (non-interleaved ⇒ each pair separated ⇒\n`c ∈ {0,2}`), which is the in-house Prop. 3.3 the target also cites.",
  "visible_reasoning": {
    "availability_description": "推理出现 MC 等价于 non-interleaved 的错误判断，同一块随后也产生怀疑；最终仍写入错误等价。不能只截怀疑句把整段说成正确。",
